{"offset": 0, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae9051f7cf7f1c7b2026140e9a673a13484d9b8b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15415", "CorpusId": 292897173}, "title": "Sensitivity or Criterion? Measuring and Moving When Language Models Act", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15415, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2341519513", "name": "Sixun Dong"}, {"authorId": "2218546567", "name": "Yebowen Hu"}, {"authorId": "2375737300", "name": "Ming Yin"}, {"authorId": "2376416480", "name": "Yi-Ran Chen"}, {"authorId": "2278462154", "name": "Fei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2304643445", "name": "Chen Chen"}], "abstract": "Before an agent acts, it must decide whether action is warranted. A single accuracy cannot distinguish failure to recognize a warranted action from reluctance to act. Signal-detection theory separates sensitivity from criterion, but criteria estimated from authored binary labels depend on each benchmark's example mix. We introduce FlipAct, a diagnostic of tool use in physical states. A rule over annotated tool properties computes each answer and a graded evidence score, fixing the Yes/No boundary at zero across models. Questions form 2-by-2 quads that hold required knowledge fixed while the state flips. The slope of the fitted Yes-rate curve measures sensitivity; its crossing at one half measures criterion. Across 44 open models, sensitivity rises with size within a family, and its ranking agrees with three of four external when-to-act benchmarks. Models respond to evidence sign rather than magnitude, accepting actions that leave the state no better about as often as helpful ones. When Yes means act, most models are conservative across rephrasings; in some, this partly reflects a preference for No. Criterion is not determined by size. Activation steering shifts criterion with weights fixed; distillation from a model's own steered copy puts the shift into its weights without a significant sensitivity change. The distilled shift transfers to six external benchmarks, including two without Yes/No answers, while criterion-free AUC is preserved on the five benchmarks where it is available. Accuracy improves where models start conservative and declines or remains within error where they start permissive. Project page: https://sixundong.com/projects/flipact"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "39c3ba1f9e49bb3e21a1f91b23645a92e7049868", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.06522", "CorpusId": 292656128}, "title": "Anatomy of LLM Sycophancy: What a Flip Rate Hides", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06522, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2345547091", "name": "Hao-Nan Huang"}, {"authorId": null, "name": "Tian-Lin Zhang"}], "abstract": "A model under pushback can correct itself, capitulate, or hold, and one flip rate counts a correction and a capitulation alike. SycoLens, a modular replay protocol, measures flip rates from replays of an item, a committed answer, and one scripted user line, each against a matched control with the line deleted. Pushback wording, committed text, answer format, boundary distance, and ground truth become factors of one instrument, where earlier instruments vary one to three, so the interactions between the factors become measurable. Across eleven frontier models and about 760,000 controlled replays, lines that assert the opposite verdict and lines that challenge without asserting one rank the models almost unrelatedly. Flip effects grow several-fold near a model's boundary, yet items the model is certain of still carry about half of the most-affected totals; planted prose anchors a commitment at rest more than it protects it. Under a yes/no readout the rankings come closer, entangled with a pressure-induced shift toward\"no\". Where a truth exists, one model re-derives and corrects itself while another abandons correct answers without written work. One score per model therefore compares different behaviours; we condense them into a reporting profile, and the records and analyses will be released upon publication."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "48c5c543ebfec1f86567dff0238ac95b81814cfe", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2608.16003", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2608-16003", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2608.16003", "CorpusId": 291183748}, "title": "Prior Audit-Repair Context Shifts LLM Verifier Thresholds Toward Leniency", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16003, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2333070727", "name": "Parsa Mazaheri"}, {"authorId": "2392720775", "name": "Kasra Mazaheri"}], "abstract": "Automated checking pipelines increasingly place one language model as the checker and another (or the same one) as the fixer. We ask whether that wiring changes what the checker reports. Measuring false alarms on human-verified-correct ProcessBench traces with the present task held byte-identical, we find that a completed audit ->repair episode already in the model's context lowers false alarms in 15 of 15 model x wording combinations, by 2.8 to 11.5 percentage points against a length-matched non-audit control, a 9 to 25% reduction relative to that control. The direction contradicts what the accumulated-message literature predicts: an episode whose audit reported an error lowers false alarms further still, at all five wordings on the model where that manipulation lands cleanly, though a negativity asymmetry predicts more flagging. Decomposing the episode finds repair content and audit verdict complementary: different components carry the effect on different model families. Signal-detection analysis locates the change in the threshold rather than in discrimination -- the criterion moves in 15 of 15 combinations and survives correction in 13 while d'survives in none, though the d'test is half as sensitive by construction -- and a hand audit of 50 false alarms finds 82% simply wrong, so at this operating point the shift need not be harmful. With reasoning enabled the effect keeps its relative size on both models tested, and the threshold reading holds there too."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7083039013647e176eda5d52d58f76d23a791ede", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2607-11881", "ArXiv": "2607.11881", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2607.11881", "CorpusId": 290142643}, "title": "Metacognition in LLMs: Foundations, Progress, and Opportunities", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.11881, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2328852241", "name": "Gabrielle Kaili-May Liu"}, {"authorId": "2114282496", "name": "Areeb Gani"}, {"authorId": "2110084666", "name": "Jacqueline Lu"}, {"authorId": "2196394816", "name": "J. Thomas"}, {"authorId": "1804885", "name": "M. Steyvers"}, {"authorId": "2355006955", "name": "Arman Cohan"}], "abstract": "Metacognition is a foundational component of intelligence critical to effective learning, problem solving, decision-making, communication, and more. In recent years, it has become increasingly recognized as a cornerstone of capable, transparent AI systems. Yet while LLMs have made significant progress across diverse real-world tasks, it is not yet clear when, how, or to what extent they can exhibit or be endowed with effective metacognitive abilities, nor how such abilities can be adapted to advance the fundamental capabilities, reliability, and intelligence of AI systems. This paper bridges this gap by presenting the first comprehensive overview of the current state of knowledge on metacognition for LLMs. We analyze and taxonomize the landscape of this emerging field and summarize recent technical advancements, including methods and benchmarks to measure and evaluate LLMs'metacognitive abilities, techniques to elicit, improve, and apply metacognition in LLMs, and findings and implications of ongoing research. We also discuss applications, open questions and challenges, and promising directions for future work. Our aim is to provide a detailed and up-to-date review of this topic and stimulate meaningful research and discussion. An organized list of papers can be found at https://github.com/yale-nlp/LLM-Metacognition."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7754355b5ea84b02b1aa0cbec64c28d8caab2335", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2606.15610", "CorpusId": 292895612}, "title": "LLM Judges Have Dark Current: Stratified Null and Utility Tests for LLM Judge Reliability", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15610, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "4556507", "name": "Hiroyasu Usami"}, {"authorId": "2118688627", "name": "K. Hara"}, {"authorId": "2442801689", "name": "Ayato Tsuboi"}, {"authorId": "2310131964", "name": "Naohiko Matsuda"}], "abstract": "Passing an exact-clone null gate does not entail passing a matched target-selection utility gate. We call a non-tie preference between exact duplicates dark current. On 102 prespecified fresh RewardBench 2 rows, all four configuration--criterion cells report 0/102 clone preferences. Both neutral utility arms pass, but strict utility lower bounds of .539 and .745 miss the .75 working threshold. Conversely, re-evaluation of the same rows on two additional configurations yields a neutral gpt-oss utility pass with 2/102 clone preferences, leaving low-null behavior uncertified. A separately frozen 485-row extension passes both aggregate working gates under both criteria on one local raw-text configuration, yet strict Precise Instruction Following (Precise IF) alignment is 58/154 (.377). Thus matched null and utility results can diverge, and aggregate passage can coexist with weak subset alignment. Interpretation is conditional on the declared configurations, fixed source mixtures, source-label targets, and study-specific working models. The bounds are marginal, the subset rate is descriptive, and non-certification does not prove a defect."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9b1110a4b5ec8c5b96047d590abaad0da2c58ced", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2605.06673", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2605-06673", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2605.06673", "CorpusId": 288110664}, "title": "Domain-level metacognitive monitoring in frontier LLMs: A 33-model atlas", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06673, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "6326414", "name": "Jon-Paul Cacioli"}], "abstract": "Aggregate metacognitive quality scores mask within-model variation across MMLU benchmark domains. We administered 1,500 MMLU items (250 per domain, under an a priori six-domain grouping) to 33 frontier LLMs from eight model families and computed Type-2 AUROC per model-domain cell using verbalized confidence (0-100). Total observations: 47,151. Every model with above-chance aggregate monitoring showed non-trivial domain-level variation. Applied/Professional knowledge was reliably the easiest benchmark domain to monitor (mean AUROC = .742, ranked top-2 in 21 of 33 models); Formal Reasoning and Natural Science were reliably the hardest (one of the two ranked bottom-2 in 27 of 33 models). The three middle domains were statistically indistinguishable (Kendall's W = .164). A subject-level coherence analysis (within-domain similarity ratio = 0.95) confirms the six-domain grouping is a pragmatic benchmark taxonomy, not a validated latent construct. Within-family profile-shape clustering is significant for Anthropic, Google-Gemini, and Qwen (permutation p<.0001) but not DeepSeek, Google-Gemma, or OpenAI. Gemma 4 31B showed a +.202 AUROC improvement over Gemma 3 27B. Three models classified Invalid on binary KEEP/WITHDRAW probes produced normal profiles under verbalized confidence, confirming probe-format specificity. Bootstrap 95% CIs on 198 cells have median width .199. Split-half aggregate stability r = .893; profile-level split-half is weaker (grand median r = .184). These results show stable benchmark-domain variation obscured by aggregate metrics, and support benchmark-stage domain screening as a step before deployment in specific application areas."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "19ad9a4295308ccd3b8ed225374a0b727d597459", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2603.25112", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2603-25112", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2603.25112", "CorpusId": 286789597}, "title": "Do LLMs Know What They Know? Measuring Metacognitive Efficiency with Signal Detection Theory", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25112, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "6326414", "name": "Jon-Paul Cacioli"}], "abstract": "Standard evaluation of LLM confidence relies on calibration metrics (ECE, Brier score) that conflate two capacities: how much a model knows (Type-1 accuracy) and how well its confidence signal tracks that knowledge (Type-2 metacognitive sensitivity). We apply Signal Detection Theory to decompose them, treating token-level normalised log-probability as a graded confidence variable and answer correctness as the state to be discriminated. We characterise the Type-2 ROC of this signal, including its unequal-variance structure via z-ROC analysis, and -- because the meta-d'efficiency ratio is not well defined for open-ended QA, which lacks a two-alternative Type-1 decision -- quantify efficiency with a model-free information measure, normalised metacognitive information (meta-I_2r). Across four LLMs and 224,000 factual QA trials we find: (1) metacognitive information varies by a factor of 1.98 across models and is not predicted by accuracy, the rank correlation being -0.80 on TriviaQA and +0.00 on Natural Questions; (2) the confidence signal has model-specific unequal-variance structure (z-ROC slopes 0.78 to 1.18) invisible to calibration metrics, the slope ordering replicating on NQ; (3) efficiency is domain-specific, weakest in Science&Technology for every model; (4) temperature dissociates accuracy from metacognitive information, which stays near-flat for three of four models while accuracy falls; and (5) metacognitive information tracks the accuracy gain from confidence-based abstention exactly (rho = +1.00) while accuracy does not. All estimates carry permutation nulls and bootstrap confidence intervals. This version (v3) corrects a differential length bias in the automated correctness scorer, validated against 1,830 human adjudications; the inverse accuracy-efficiency coupling reported in v1 and v2 does not survive relabelling. See the version note on page 1."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "32aa50cd96847d966d2441264774832cd2af0499", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2606-15610", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2606.15610", "CorpusId": 289300062}, "title": "LLM Judges Have Dark Current: A Psychometric Datasheet for LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: The following paper fields have been elided by the publisher: {'abstract'}. Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.48550/arXiv.2606.15610?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.15610, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "4556507", "name": "Hiroyasu Usami"}, {"authorId": "2118688627", "name": "K. Hara"}, {"authorId": "2442801689", "name": "Ayato Tsuboi"}, {"authorId": "2310131964", "name": "Naohiko Matsuda"}], "abstract": null}}]}
